    "question": "Which profits is the central procurement company entitled to?",
    "mcq": [
      "The profit potential that comes with the risk it assumes in buying, holding and on-selling goods, but not the profits that stem from the group's purchasing power.",
      "The profit potential that comes with the risk it assumes, plus a share of the purchasing-power benefit, because it is the group company that now places the orders.",
      "Nothing beyond its costs, because every benefit of centralised purchasing belongs to the associated enterprises whose procurement activities it replaced."
    ],
    "correctIdx": 0,
    "analysis": "Centralising purchasing in a single company to obtain volume discounts is a deliberate concerted group action, and under the guidance in Chapter I the resulting benefits should be attributed to the enterprises that contribute to creating the synergies. The central procurement company makes no contribution to creating those synergies, so the profits that stem from the group's purchasing power are not its to keep. What it is entitled to is the profit potential that comes with the risk it assumes in buying, holding and on-selling goods. How economically significant that risk is, and which party or parties assume it, is determined under the risk framework of Section D.1.2.1 of Chapter I.",
